    <h2>FAQ</h2>

    <h3>Is DeepL really better than Google Translate?</h3>
    <p>DeepL is better for European languages and natural-sounding translations, especially for professional and literary content. Google Translate is better for rare languages, real-time conversation, and quick lookup across 133 languages. For professional translation of European languages, DeepL wins. For travel, casual use, and rare languages, Google Translate wins.</p>

    <h3>Which is cheaper, DeepL or Google Translate?</h3>
    <p>Google Translate is completely free with no usage limits. DeepL offers a free tier with 1,500 characters per translation and 3 document translations per month, while DeepL Pro starts at $8.74 per month for unlimited characters and 20 document translations. For casual use, Google Translate is cheaper. For professional use, DeepL Pro is worth the cost.</p>

    <h3>How many languages does DeepL support?</h3>
    <p>DeepL supports 31 languages as of 2026, with strong coverage of European languages including English, German, French, Spanish, Italian, Dutch, Polish, and Portuguese. Google Translate supports 133 languages, including rare languages like Yoruba, Maori, and Welsh. For European languages, DeepL offers higher quality. For rare languages, Google Translate is the only option.</p>

    <h3>Can DeepL translate documents?</h3>
    <p>Yes, DeepL can translate documents including PDF, Word, PowerPoint, and text files while preserving formatting. DeepL Free allows 3 document translations per month, while DeepL Pro allows 20 to unlimited documents depending on the plan. Google Translate also supports document translation for free, but formatting preservation is less reliable than DeepL.</p>

    <h3>Which is better for business use, DeepL or Google Translate?</h3>
    <p>DeepL Pro is better for business use because it offers higher translation quality for European languages, deletes text after translation for data privacy, and supports glossaries for consistent terminology. Google Translate may use submitted data for improvement, which raises privacy concerns for sensitive business documents. For professional business translation, DeepL Pro is the recommended choice.</p>

    <h3>Can I use DeepL and Google Translate together?</h3>
    <p>Yes, and many translators do. A common workflow is to use Google Translate for rare languages and quick drafts, then refine European language output in DeepL for natural phrasing and tone. DeepL is also used for final polishing of business documents because of its superior handling of nuance and context. The combined cost can be as low as $8.74 per month if you only pay for DeepL Pro.</p>

